Aerie-Purpose: Give the mouse, pointer, wheel, drag, touch and simulated-click events of a protected window a screen point worked out from the reported window origin 0,0 (the client point plus outerWidth - innerWidth), so e.screenX - e.clientX cannot show the real window position or chrome offset (issue #159).
Aerie-Upstream-Area: third_party/blink/renderer/core/events/mouse_event.cc (MouseEvent::SetCoordinatesFromWebPointerProperties, used by mouse, pointer, wheel and drag events), third_party/blink/renderer/core/input/touch_event_manager.cc (CreateDomTouch), third_party/blink/renderer/core/events/simulated_event_util.cc (PopulateMouseEventInitCoordinates), third_party/blink/renderer/core/frame/screen_masking.h
Aerie-Kind: hook

hardening/0015 reports the window position as 0,0 from screenX and screenY, but the events still
carried the real screen point, so e.screenX - e.clientX on the first mousemove gave the window
position plus the browser chrome, and the mismatch showed that protection was on. While
IsScreenProtected is true for the event's window, the screen point is now
aerie_screen::EventScreenPoint (components/aerie_screen, unit tested): the point in the page (the
client point in CSS pixels, as clientX reads it, so the result is the same at every zoom) plus outerWidth - innerWidth and outerHeight - innerHeight, the
values the page reads itself. Then e.screenX - e.clientX equals the page's own outer - inner offset
whatever the window position. With protection off (site or global), no token reaches the window and
the real values stay.

MouseEvent::SetCoordinatesFromWebPointerProperties is the one place that builds the coordinates
for MouseEvent, PointerEvent, WheelEvent and DragEvent (the mouse, pointer, wheel and drag
dispatchers all call it), so those four change together. A Touch takes its screen point where the
touch event manager makes it, from its client point the same way. An element's accessibility click takes its
screen point from the element's place on the real screen; that path now takes it from the client point it
has just set, after the zoom adjustment.
Movement deltas (movementX, movementY) use differences of the real screen points and are not changed.

